---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import PregnancyCalculator from '@components/islands/PregnancyCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('health', 'pregnancy-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How far along am I?', answer: 'Pregnancy is dated from the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP), not conception. Choose how you are dating the pregnancy — a due date, your last period, an ultrasound, a conception date or an IVF transfer — and the calculator shows your current week and day, trimester, and progress toward your due date.' },
  { question: 'I already have a due date. Can I use it?', answer: 'Yes — choose Due Date and enter it. The calculator works the whole schedule backwards from that date, so if your provider revised your dating after a scan, everything here matches what they told you rather than a fresh estimate that might disagree.' },
  { question: 'Which dating method is the most accurate?', answer: 'An IVF transfer is the most precise, because the embryo\'s age on transfer day is known exactly. A first-trimester ultrasound comes next, since the scan measures the pregnancy rather than inferring it. Last period is the conventional route and is reliable when cycles are regular; conception date is only as good as your knowledge of the date.' },
  { question: 'What are the trimesters?', answer: 'The first trimester runs from week 1 to the end of week 13, the second from week 14 to the end of week 27, and the third from week 28 until birth. Each brings different developments and milestones.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between gestational age and fetal age?', answer: 'Gestational age is counted from your last period and is the standard used in prenatal care; fetal (or conceptional) age is counted from conception and is about two weeks less. "40 weeks pregnant" by gestational age is roughly 38 weeks since conception.' },
  { question: 'When did I conceive?', answer: 'Conception typically occurs about two weeks after the LMP, around ovulation. The calculator shows this estimated date, though the exact day varies with cycle length and ovulation timing.' },
  { question: 'How many weeks is a full-term pregnancy?', answer: 'Full term is 39–40 weeks, though birth any time from 37 to 42 weeks is considered normal. The 40-week due date is the midpoint of that range, not a deadline.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-25' }} intro="Track your pregnancy week by week — from a due date, your last period, an ultrasound, a conception date or an IVF transfer. See how far along you are today and the whole schedule ahead." faqs={faqs}>
  <PregnancyCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose how you are dating the pregnancy: <strong>Due Date</strong>, <strong>Last Period</strong>, <strong>Ultrasound</strong>, <strong>Conception Date</strong> or <strong>IVF Transfer Date</strong>. Only that method's fields appear.</li>
      <li>Fill them in. Under Last Period you can also give your <strong>average cycle length</strong> if it is not the textbook 28 days.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate Pregnancy Progress</strong> to see how many <strong>weeks and days</strong> along you are today, your <strong>trimester</strong>, a progress bar toward your due date, and the whole <strong>schedule</strong> with today's position marked in it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The five ways to date a pregnancy</h2>
    <p>
      Four of these estimate the due date; the fifth starts from one you already have. They all
      converge on the same thing — a last menstrual period, from which every other date follows —
      but they differ in how much guesswork stands in the way, so they are not equally certain.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Due Date</strong> is for when your provider has already given you one, often revised after a dating scan. The calculator runs the 280-day rule backwards and builds the schedule from there, so nothing here contradicts what you were told.</li>
      <li><strong>Last Period</strong> is the conventional route and the one prenatal care starts from, because the LMP is a date most people can name. It assumes ovulation around day 14, which is why a cycle that is not 28 days shifts everything.</li>
      <li><strong>Ultrasound</strong> is the most reliable estimate early on, because the scan measures the pregnancy rather than inferring it from a cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Conception Date</strong> counts 266 days forward instead of 280 — the same rule, started two weeks later. Useful if you know the date, which most people do not.</li>
      <li><strong>IVF Transfer Date</strong> is the most precise of the five: the embryo's age on transfer day is known exactly, so a 5-day transfer is due 261 days later and a 3-day transfer 263.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How pregnancy is dated</h2>
    <p>
      By long-standing convention, pregnancy is counted from the first day of your last period —
      about two weeks before conception actually occurs. The LMP is used because it is a date most
      people can pinpoint, while the exact moment of conception usually cannot be known. Every
      prenatal test and milestone is scheduled against this gestational timeline.
    </p>

    <h2>Gestational age versus fetal age</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Gestational age</strong> is measured from the LMP and is the standard used in prenatal
      care. <strong>Fetal age</strong> (or conceptional age) is counted from conception and runs
      about two weeks less. So "40 weeks pregnant" by gestational age is roughly 38 weeks since
      conception — the same pregnancy, two different starting lines.
    </p>

    <h2>The key dates</h2>
    <p>All the dates derive from your LMP:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Due date</strong> = LMP + 280 days (Naegele's rule)</li>
      <li><strong>Estimated conception</strong> = LMP + 14 days</li>
      <li><strong>End of the first trimester</strong> = end of week 13 (LMP + 91 days)</li>
      <li><strong>End of the second trimester</strong> = end of week 27 (LMP + 189 days)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Say your last period began on <strong>January 1, 2026</strong>. Conception is estimated around
      <strong>January 15</strong>, the first trimester ends about <strong>April 2</strong>, the
      second ends about <strong>July 9</strong>, and your due date is <strong>October 8, 2026</strong>.
      If today were June 1, you would be <strong>21 weeks and 4 days</strong> along — in the second
      trimester, just past the halfway mark.
    </p>

    <h2>The three trimesters</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>First (weeks 1–13):</strong> major organs form; early symptoms and the highest rate of developmental change.</li>
      <li><strong>Second (weeks 14–27):</strong> often the most comfortable stretch, and when movement usually becomes noticeable.</li>
      <li><strong>Third (weeks 28–birth):</strong> rapid growth and preparation for delivery.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What full term means</h2>
    <p>
      Full term is <strong>39–40 weeks</strong>, and birth any time from <strong>37 to 42 weeks</strong>
      is considered normal, so the 40-week due date is the midpoint of a range rather than a deadline.
      Only about 4% of babies arrive on the exact date. For the due date on its own, use the
      <a href="/health/due-date-calculator">due date calculator</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything here is educational. Your healthcare provider tracks your pregnancy with
      examinations and ultrasounds and can adjust these dates to your individual situation — and
      if they have already given you a due date, enter that one and the schedule will follow it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
